Okay, so the plan is to create a producer and a consumer architecture. The producer runs an async worker task in the background and its whole job is to iterate through the graph.astream and push every chunk into an asyncio.Queue. Then we have the consumer, it's a while True loop that puts an async with asyncio.timeout(10) that pulls items fromm queue.get() and feeds it to the SSE generator. I.e., we yield here.

Some pseudocode could be like this:
async producer:
	iterate through graph.astream and await queue.put(chunk)

async consumer:
	while true, queue.get(chunk) with timeout 10. If failure (TimeoutError) yield a ping
	on success yield the actual chunk data logic or whatever.

	
create task for producer
await consumer

All of this will go inside my @app.post() function because this should run automatically when someone POSTs to the /api/message endpoint.

Done! I am so fucking back. I discussed this with an LLM too because I had made three errors initially:
1. I was not returning a StreamingResponse object, I was just returning an AsyncIterable, so fastapi was crashing.
2. I was using a global queue - which meant that two users who hit the endpoint at different times will be connected to the same session.
3. I was not adding a sentinel/final value to my loop when the astream from langgraph ended. This was a perfect use case for finally. Then the consumer just checks if this there and quits the while loop.

Apart from that my event loop was fucking blocked again because of that OCR task which was taking 20+ seconds. The solution for that was just calling a stream writer before the heavy OCR job and assigning it its own separate thread so that I still receive status updates.

As for cognitive losses, I knew no shit about async programming and did some things to get here. I feel like my brain is fried - I could've seen the global queue thing and how that would cause a problem. And the sentinel thing too. I am still not accustomed to async programming. I took an LLM's help here; 3/10.

I need to write about async programming. It's fucking magic when it works.
